  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "The offense never arrived. Warren gave them a clean five-plus innings and the bullpen held the back end clean, but three hits and seven strikeouts against Boston cost them the game before it got started.",
    "what_this_means": "This team doesn't score one run by accident. It happens when the bullpen carries too much weight and the lineup goes silent. Which is exactly what happened here. Warren's line was good enough to win in most ballparks, but the Yankees needed him to be great because the bats were already asleep by the second inning. Boston scored twice in that inning and the game was decided there. Yarbrough and De los Santos both worked clean innings after, but pitching alone doesn't win games when your offense leaves nine strikeouts on the board. This is what fragile looks like. Not a collapse, but a failure to show up at all. They're one loss back in the division with a lineup that went cold at exactly the wrong moment against the team directly behind them in the standings. That margin doesn't hold if the bats stay silent.",
    "what_to_watch": "Jake Bennett takes the mound tonight with a 3.71 ERA. Cole is ready to answer.",
